Start here
==========

This is a Windows port of cadaver (https://github.com/notroj/cadaver).
If you are picking it up cold, read these in order:

  1. README.md    what this fork is, how to build it, how to run it
  2. AGENTS.md    how to drive cadaver from a program, and how to read
                  what it prints back
  3. PATCHES.md   what was changed inside neon/ and why, needed before
                  you update the bundled neon
  4. This file    what is left to do

Layout:

  src/            cadaver itself: the command implementations
                  (commands.c), the command table and dispatcher
                  (cadaver.c), the tokeniser and glob driver
                  (cmdline.c), options (options.c), listings (ls.c),
                  the edit command (edit.c), DASL search (search.c)
                  and DeltaV (version.c)
  lib/            portability: the platform layer (system.c), filename
                  globbing (glob.c), the password prompt (getpass.c),
                  temporary files (mkstemp.c), .netrc (netrc.c)
  neon/           vendored neon 0.37.1, checked in, not a submodule.
                  Patched; see PATCHES.md
  win32/config.h  generated config.h, checked in so Makefile.w32 works
                  without autotools.  win32/regen-config.sh rewrites it
  VERSION         the fork version, read by both build paths
  tests/          the four test harnesses and their expected output
  Makefile.w32    the no-autotools build
  doc/cadaver.1   the manual page
  .github/        CI: lint, both build paths, four gates, release

Everything is one executable:

  cadaver URL
  cadaver -r commands.txt URL
  printf 'ls\nquit\n' | cadaver URL

Test harnesses in tests/:

  offline.sh      18 checks that need no server, no network and no
                  Python.  Run it first; it takes under a second.
  glob.sh         48 unit tests for lib/glob.c, compiled on the spot.
                  Run it after touching the globbing.
  godav.sh        the scripted sessions against golang.org/x/net/webdav
  wsgidav.sh      the same sessions against wsgidav
  session.sh      the driver both of those call: runs each
                  tests/sessions/*.cad and compares the transcript
                  against tests/expected/<server>/
  normalise.py    replaces the parts of a transcript that legitimately
                  differ between runs
  expand.py       substitutes the paths into a session script

Neither test server is complete, and the difference matters:

  * x/net/webdav locks correctly, including a LOCK on an unmapped URL,
    but has no dead property store, so most of the props session fails
    against it.
  * wsgidav has a dead property store, so props is meaningful there,
    but it answers LOCK with the Content-Type "application;
    charset=utf-8", which is not a media type.  neon discards the body
    unparsed, cadaver never sees the lock token, and every lock test
    reports a failure that is the server's.

So: verify lock work against godav.sh, property work against
wsgidav.sh, and do not read either result as the whole picture.

Before you change anything
==========================

Run the gates and note the numbers:

  ./tests/offline.sh
  ./tests/glob.sh
  ./tests/godav.sh
  ./tests/wsgidav.sh

All four must pass, the last two printing "All sessions match the
expected results".  If they do not, fix that before doing anything
else.  Everything below assumes a green baseline.

Both build paths must stay warning-free:

  ./autogen.sh && ./configure --with-ssl=openssl --enable-warnings && make
  make -f Makefile.w32

CI greps the build log for "warning:" and fails on a hit, so a new
warning is a regression rather than background noise.

Conventions, in code and in prose:

  * Binary units, always: KiB, MiB, GiB, powers of 1024.  Never KB, MB
    or GB for a quantity of bytes, and never a decimal power of ten.
    Rates are MiB/s.
  * Timestamps in anything machine-readable are ISO 8601 in UTC with
    millisecond precision and a trailing Z, e.g.
    2026-08-19T13:04:54.429Z.  Truncate sub-second digits, do not
    round, so a stamp never names a moment that had not happened yet.
  * Durations are seconds to millisecond resolution, and are
    wall-clock.  Say so when reporting them, because they include
    server and network time.
  * Quote exact byte counts where the exact figure matters, with the
    rounded binary figure in brackets after it.
  * The version lives in VERSION and nowhere else.  win32/config.h
    deliberately has no copy of it.

If you change what cadaver prints, the expected transcripts have to
move with it:

  ./tests/godav.sh --regenerate
  ./tests/wsgidav.sh --regenerate

and then read the diff before committing it.  --regenerate will
happily enshrine a regression; the point of those files is that a
change in behaviour has to be looked at by a person.

High value work
===============

In rough order.  The first three are what stands between this and
being usable from a script without heuristics.

* The exit status says nothing.  main() returns 0 however the session
  went: every command can fail, the connection can be refused, and
  cadaver still exits 0.  A script has to parse the transcript, which
  AGENTS.md documents because there is currently no alternative.

  What it should be: 0 if every command succeeded, non-zero if any
  failed, with the count capped so it cannot be mistaken for a
  signalled exit.  out_result() in src/commands.c is the one place
  every command's outcome passes through, so the counter goes there.
  Decide what a command that prints its own failure without going
  through out_result() should do -- rmcol on a plain resource, for
  instance -- and make them all consistent.

  While you are there: --help and --version exit -1, which Windows
  reports as 4294967295.  Both should exit 0, and a usage error 2.

* No machine-readable output.  litmus-windows grew --json for exactly
  this reason and the shape it settled on is worth copying: one object
  on stdout and nothing else, with the human-readable message kept as
  a "context" string and a separate "error" object carrying the method,
  the request target as it went on the wire, and the status, so that a
  consumer branches on the classification rather than on prose.

  For cadaver the natural unit is the command rather than the test:

    {"command": "put", "args": ["report.pdf"],
     "target": "/dav/report.pdf", "status": "ok", "duration": 0.061}

  with a run-level object around the sequence.  Design it once and
  document it in AGENTS.md as a contract, as litmus-windows does.

* No wire trace.  `set debug http` exists and goes to stderr, which is
  most of the way there, but it is neon's format, it cannot be sent to
  a file, and it cannot be turned on from the command line.  A
  --trace[=FILE] that dumps every request and response, tagged with the
  command that issued it, would make a failing session diagnosable
  without a proxy.  litmus-windows has one; the mechanism is
  ne_debug_init() plus ne_hook_create_request/ne_hook_post_headers.

* A failed `get' leaves an empty file behind.  do_get() in
  src/commands.c opens the local file with O_CREAT before making the
  request, so a 404 or a dropped connection leaves a zero-length file.
  The next `get' of the same resource then finds it, prompts "Enter
  local filename", reads end of file in a script, and downloads
  nothing.  One transient error thus poisons every retry.

  Download to a temporary name in the same directory and rename on
  success, or unlink on failure if the file was created by this call.

* `get' prompts when the local file exists.  Reasonable interactively,
  useless in a script: with standard input at end of file it prints
  "cancelled." and nothing is downloaded.  There should be a way to
  say what to do -- an --overwrite or --no-clobber flag, or an option
  in the same family as `overwrite'.

* `resumeget' can corrupt the local file.  neon's ne_get_range()
  checks that the response was 206 and reports an error otherwise, but
  it checks after the body has already been written to the descriptor.
  A server that ignores Range and answers 200 with the whole resource
  therefore appends a complete copy to the partial file, and cadaver
  reports a failure without saying that the file is now wrong.  Record
  the size before the request and truncate back to it on failure.

* `mput *' tries to upload directories.  It reports "Could not open
  file: Permission denied" for each one, which is neither accurate nor
  useful.  Skip a directory with a clear message, or add a recursive
  upload -- see rget/rput below.

Inherited upstream issues
=========================

Every open issue in notroj/cadaver at the time of the fork, with what
is actually wrong.  Upstream has not moved on any of them; they are
this fork's to fix.

* #75, netrc parser removes single quote from value (open since
  2026-04-27).  lib/netrc.c strips any ' or " it finds while reading a
  token, so a password containing one is silently mangled and
  authentication fails with no indication why.  The reporter's password
  was randomly generated and worked with every other WebDAV client.

  The netrc convention is that a quote only quotes when it opens a
  token; inside one it is an ordinary character.  Fix the tokeniser to
  match, and add a case to the tests -- this is testable offline
  against a temporary .netrc, so it wants a harness of its own rather
  than a session test.

* #64, lock owner is not XML-escaped (open since 2025-05-29).
  `set lockowner 'foo&bar'` then `lock` produces 400 Bad Request.
  getowner() in src/commands.c wraps the option's value in <href> and
  hands the result to neon, and neon's ne_locks.c concatenates it into
  the request body verbatim:

    ne_buffer_concat(body, "<owner>", lock->owner, "</owner>\n", NULL);

  so an ampersand or an angle bracket in the value goes on the wire as
  itself and the body is not well-formed XML.  neon has no escaping
  helper to call -- there is no ne_xml_escape -- so cadaver has to do
  it: escape &, <, > and the quotes in getowner(), and add a session
  that sets an owner containing them.  The same body escaping arguably
  belongs in neon; worth reporting there as well.

* #54, `mv collection/ newname/' fails (open since 2024-11-27, reported
  by the upstream maintainer).  do_copymove() refuses a collection
  source with a non-collection destination, which is right when the
  destination exists and is a plain resource, but a name that does not
  exist yet is not a non-collection.  Renaming a collection with `mv'
  worked before 0.26.  `rename' is the workaround.

  Distinguish "the destination exists and is not a collection", which
  must still be refused, from "the destination does not exist", which
  is a rename.

* #25, automatic login with .cadaverrc and .netrc not working (open
  since 2023-01-10).  Not a parser bug: supply_creds_server() in
  src/cadaver.c uses the netrc entry only when it has both an account
  and a password, so a .netrc that supplies the username and expects a
  prompt for the password is ignored entirely, and both are asked for.
  Use whichever fields are present and prompt for the rest.

* #4, cannot access a share whose resourcetype is malformed (open since
  2022-07-15).  A server returned <collection/> without the DAV
  namespace prefix, so cadaver did not recognise the collection and
  reported "(not WebDAV-enabled?)".  The server is wrong and the
  reporter could not get it fixed.  Upstream left the issue open to
  improve the message.

  getrestype() in src/utils.c is where it lands.  Say what was actually
  wrong -- that the resource exists but did not report itself as a
  collection -- and point at -t, which is the workaround.

* #58 is not a bug report; it is an offer of help from a university
  open source programme office.  Nothing to do.

Upstream pull requests
======================

* #74, "Misc changes", open.  Three parts.  The neon update to 0.37.1
  is already here.  The other two are worth taking:

   - src/commands.c: propname_iterator() prints its names with a plain
     space, where `propget' and `head' use the bullet.  Making them
     consistent changes the expected transcripts, so do it with
     --regenerate and read the diff.
   - src/cadaver.c: a ne_version_match(0, 29) check at startup.  With a
     statically linked bundled neon this can never fire, so it is
     documentation rather than a check -- take it only if the tree ever
     grows a way to build against a system neon.

  Every other pull request upstream is merged or closed.

Found while porting
===================

Things this fork noticed and did not fix, because they are behaviour
changes rather than portability work.

* `unlock' on a resource with no stored lock prompts "Enter locktoken:"
  and, at end of input, gives up without a word: no message, no failure
  line, nothing in the transcript to distinguish it from success.  See
  execute_unlock() in src/commands.c, the `goto unlock_fail' path.

* `discover' reports "no locks found" whether the server returned an
  empty lockdiscovery or did not support the request.  Those deserve
  different messages.

* `put' names its destination in URI form and every other command names
  it in native form.  Uploading a file called "has space.txt" reports

    Uploading has space.txt to `/paths/has%20space.txt'

  where `delete' on the same resource reports `/paths/has space.txt'.
  simple_put() passes the already-resolved URI path to output()
  directly; every other command goes through out_start_uri(), which
  converts it back.  tests/expected/*/paths.txt records the current
  behaviour, so fixing it means regenerating those.

* Failure paths leak.  Most commands build URLs with ne_concat and
  return early without freeing them.  It is memory only, in a process
  that is about to read another command rather than exit, so a long
  session with many failures grows.  Cleaning it up properly means a
  cleanup path per command.

* `lls' accepts only -l.  It is cadaver's own listing now rather than a
  forked ls, so anything else -- -a, -t, -S -- has to be implemented
  rather than passed on.  -a is the one worth having: the listing hides
  nothing today, so -a would have to become the non-default.

* The progress indicator prints one dot per transfer callback when the
  output is not a terminal, so a large transfer produces a line of
  hundreds of dots in a log.  tests/normalise.py collapses them for the
  expected transcripts.  Consider a byte count instead when the output
  is not a terminal.

* Tab completion has not been exercised in a real Windows console.  It
  works when input is piped, which is what the tests cover, and
  readline 8.3 from MSYS2 does support the Windows console, but nobody
  has sat in front of cmd.exe and pressed Tab through a remote
  completion.  Do that before the first release.

* The `search' and DeltaV commands (version, checkin, checkout,
  uncheckout, history, label) are exercised by neither test server,
  because neither implements DASL or DeltaV.  They are compiled and
  linked and otherwise unverified.  Finding a third server that does
  would be the way to cover them; until then, treat any change there as
  untested.

From upstream's own TODO
========================

Upstream's TODO was mostly a wish list from the early 2000s.  What is
still worth doing:

* `rget' and `rput' for recursive download and upload, creating
  collections as needed.  This is the most-requested missing feature
  and the reason people reach for sitecopy or rclone instead.

* Abort a transfer in progress.  Ctrl-C during a `get' of a large file
  currently kills cadaver.

* `copy' and `move' over an existing resource fail with 412 rather than
  prompting or reporting clearly.

* `ls' is inflexible: one format, one collection at a time, no way to
  choose which properties are shown.  A --format or a `set lsformat'
  would make it useful for scripts -- although a proper --json output
  would serve that better, so do that first.

* A resource-state cache for the duration of one command.  cadaver
  makes a PROPFIND to decide whether a path is a collection, and does
  it repeatedly within one command: `copy a b c dest' resolves the
  destination once and every source separately.  A small hash keyed by
  URI, discarded at the end of each command, would cut the request
  count noticeably on a slow link.

* Upload on save from the editor, so that `edit' with a long-running
  editor pushes each save rather than only the final one.  Requested in
  2001 and never done; a file watcher would do it now.

Things that were looked at and deliberately not done
====================================================

* Keeping NLS.  cadaver had Spanish and Italian translations and a
  gettext build.  A single self-contained executable cannot carry a
  locale directory, and a translated build would need the .mo files
  installed next to it, which is the opposite of what this fork is for.
  The _() and N_() macros are still in the source and resolve to the
  identity, so no message has been lost.  Bringing it back means
  restoring the po directory and its Makefile.in.in from upstream's
  history, the gettext and iconv macros in m4, AM_GNU_GETTEXT in
  configure.ac, and a LOCALEDIR for src/cadaver.c to hand
  bindtextdomain() -- that call is still there under #ifdef ENABLE_NLS
  and would not compile without one.

* Keeping the bundled glibc glob.  It is 35707 bytes (34.9 KiB) of
  1998 glibc, needs fnmatch, pwd.h and a d_type that Windows does not
  have, and its interrupt support was inside #ifdef SHELL, which was
  never defined -- so the Ctrl-C handling cadaver installed around it
  had never worked.  lib/glob.c is a third the size, has unit tests,
  and handles Windows separators and case-insensitivity.

* Emitting LF rather than CRLF on standard output.  cadaver writes its
  output in text mode, so a Windows console and a Windows editor both
  see what they expect.  Transfers are unaffected: `put', `get' and
  `edit' are binary throughout, and `cat' switches its own output to
  binary for the length of the transfer.  tests/normalise.py strips
  carriage returns, so the expected transcripts do not depend on it.

* Sorting `ls' output differently.  ls.c sorts errors first, then
  collections, then by URI, which is what upstream does and what the
  expected transcripts record.  Changing it is a user-visible choice
  that should be a `set' option rather than a new default.

* An MSVC build.  Everything here assumes GCC: the warning set, the
  __attribute__ annotations in neon, the makefiles.  MinGW-w64 produces
  a native Windows binary with no runtime dependency on MSYS2, which is
  what the port needed; MSVC would add a second toolchain to support
  for no gain the users can see.

Cutting a release
=================

Bump VERSION, regenerate the checked-in configuration if the neon
version or anything else configure records has moved:

  ./win32/regen-config.sh

commit, wait for CI to go green on that commit, then

  git tag -a vX.Y-winN
  git push origin vX.Y-winN

The tag must be annotated and its name must be "v" plus VERSION
exactly, or the release job refuses to run.  It also refuses if a
release with that tag already exists, and it checks both before it
uploads anything, so a good release cannot be overwritten by accident.
The workflow copies the tag's message above the generated notes, so the
tag message is the release description.

Do not quote the built executable's size in a tag message: it is not
known until CI has built it, and it moves with the OpenSSL, expat and
readline versions the runner happens to have.

Afterwards, confirm the fixed URL resolves and the binary runs:

  curl -fsSL -o cadaver.exe https://github.com/Azathothas/cadaver-windows/releases/latest/download/cadaver.exe
  ./cadaver.exe --version
